PG.notes("l001", "<p class=\"summary\">Promotional course trailer. It outlines the scope of the bootcamp (HTML through to React and the back end), the project-based format, and who the course is aimed at. No code is shown.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Instructor: Angela Yu, lead instructor at the London App Brewery. The online course follows the same curriculum as their 12-week in-person bootcamp.</li><li>Format: roughly 55+ hours of video plus step-by-step tutorials, interactive coding exercises and quizzes.</li><li>Scope runs from front end (HTML, CSS, Bootstrap, JavaScript) to back end (Node) and React, with the MERN stack mentioned as part of the full-stack path.</li><li>Everything is taught through projects. Examples named in the trailer: a product website built with HTML/CSS/Bootstrap, games, a drum-kit music app, plus portfolio-friendly projects such as a to-do list and a project-management tool.</li><li>No prior programming experience is assumed. Experienced developers are told to check the downloadable curriculum and start at whatever section fits their level.</li><li>The stated aim is to teach the reasoning behind each step as well as the steps themselves.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l002", "<p class=\"summary\">Short resource page with a single button linking to a PDF of the full course syllabus (hosted on Google Drive). No teaching content.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>The syllabus PDF lists every topic in the bootcamp. Use it to see where each module sits and to choose a starting point if you already know some of the material.</li><li>The download is a Google Drive link. If it fails, the article explains that heavy traffic is the usual cause and a later retry normally works.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A copy of the syllabus is also in the reference repo as 'Section 01 - Front-End Web Development/01.01 - Web Dev Syllabus.pdf'.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's folder names give a quick map of the course order: HTML, intermediate HTML, multi-page sites, CSS (intro, properties, intermediate, advanced), Flexbox, Grid, Bootstrap, web design, a personal-site capstone, JavaScript ES6, DOM, two 'boss level' games (Dicee, Simon), jQuery, Unix command line, Node, Express, EJS, Git/GitHub, APIs, SQL/PostgreSQL, authentication, React, then Web3/ICP DApps.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l003", "<p class=\"summary\">Resource page offering a free eBook of twelve study habits the instructor has seen in her strongest students. The page itself is a short motivational note plus a download link; it contains no technical content.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>The eBook collects 12 study tips for learning to code, drawn from the App Brewery's experience teaching bootcamp students.</li><li>The download link sits at the bottom of the lecture page (visible once enrolled).</li><li>The Udemy mobile app may not handle the download; open the lecture on a desktop browser to get the PDF.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A copy is in the reference repo at 'Section 01 - Front-End Web Development/01.02 - 12 Rules to Learn to Code.pdf', alongside a Cornell-notes template PDF (01.03) that pairs with the note-taking advice in lecture 7.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Trying to download from the Udemy mobile app instead of a desktop browser.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l004", "<p class=\"summary\">Checklist of the tools used for the front-end part of the course: the VS Code editor, three VS Code extensions (Live Preview, Prettier, vscode-icons) and the Google Chrome browser for its developer tools.</p>\n<h3>Code editor: Visual Studio Code</h3>\n<ul><li>VS Code is Microsoft's free editor and is where all course code is written. Download from code.visualstudio.com.</li><li>Some older videos show the Atom editor. Atom has been retired by GitHub, so wherever a video says to open Atom or do something in Atom, do the same thing in VS Code instead.</li></ul>\n<h3>VS Code extensions to install</h3>\n<ul><li>Extensions are add-ons that give VS Code extra features. Each link in the article opens the Visual Studio Marketplace page; clicking Install there hands off to the VS Code app.</li><li>Live Preview (Microsoft, marketplace id ms-vscode.live-server): shows an HTML file in a browser pane inside VS Code and refreshes as you save. Used from the next section onward to view exercise files (right-click index.html → Show Preview).</li><li>Prettier (esbenp.prettier-vscode): an automatic code formatter that fixes indentation and spacing so files stay tidy and readable.</li><li>vscode-icons (vscode-icons-team.vscode-icons): replaces the generic file icons in the Explorer sidebar with per-file-type icons, which makes .html, .css and .js files easier to tell apart.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> You can also install from inside VS Code: open the Extensions view (Cmd+Shift+X on Mac, Ctrl+Shift+X on Windows/Linux), search the name, check the publisher, click Install.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> To have Prettier run every time you save, enable Settings → 'Format On Save' and set 'Default Formatter' to Prettier. This is why files can re-indent themselves on save in later lessons.</li></ul>\n<h3>Browser: Google Chrome</h3>\n<ul><li>Chrome is free and is the browser used throughout the course, mainly for Chrome DevTools, which later lessons rely on heavily (inspecting HTML/CSS, the JavaScript console, etc.).</li><li>Install it even if you normally browse with something else, so your screen matches the videos.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> DevTools opens with F12, Cmd+Option+I (Mac) or Ctrl+Shift+I (Windows/Linux), or right-click → Inspect.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Set up the development environment</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A computer without the course tools installed.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Have VS Code with the three extensions, plus Chrome, ready before the HTML section begins.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Download and install VS Code from code.visualstudio.com.</li><li>Install the Live Preview extension (publisher Microsoft).</li><li>Install the Prettier code formatter extension.</li><li>Install the vscode-icons extension.</li><li>Download and install Google Chrome.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Check it works: open any folder in VS Code, create index.html, right-click it and confirm 'Show Preview' appears.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> VS Code opens, the Explorer shows file-type icons, a right-click on an HTML file offers Show Preview, and Chrome is installed with DevTools available.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Following old videos that mention Atom; use VS Code for everything.</li><li>Installing a different 'Live Server' style extension instead of Microsoft's Live Preview, then not finding the Show Preview option used in later lessons.</li><li>Skipping Chrome and then finding DevTools steps look different in another browser.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l005", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains the internet as a physical network of cables linking computers, the client/server roles, and the path a request takes when you type a domain name: browser → ISP → DNS server → IP address → direct request to the server, which sends back the site's files. Ends with a hands-on DNS lookup and a look at the undersea cables that carry the traffic.</p>\n<h3>The internet is physical wiring</h3>\n<ul><li>Strip away the 'cloud' image: the internet is a very large set of wires (copper and fibre) joining computers together so they can exchange data, for example a machine in London talking to one in Seattle.</li><li>It is the same idea as wiring two computers together, scaled up to every connected computer on the planet.</li></ul>\n<h3>Servers and clients</h3>\n<ul><li>Server: a computer whose job is to stay online around the clock and hand out the files and data a website needs whenever someone asks for them.</li><li>Client: any computer (laptop, phone, etc.) that a person uses to go online and request those files.</li><li>A server can be pictured as a library that never closes: you ask for a page (Google's home page, the latest articles on a news site) and it supplies everything needed to display it.</li><li>The catch is scale: with so many sites stored on so many machines, the client needs a way to find the exact machine that holds the site it wants. DNS and IP addresses solve that.</li></ul>\n<h3>What happens when you type google.com</h3>\n<ul><li>1. The browser sends the request to your ISP (Internet Service Provider), the company you pay for access. Examples given: AT&amp;T or Comcast in the US, BT or TalkTalk in the UK.</li><li>2. The ISP passes the domain name on to a DNS server. DNS stands for Domain Name System.</li><li>3. The DNS server works like an enhanced phone book: it looks up the domain name in its database and finds the matching IP address.</li><li>4. The IP address travels back through the ISP to your browser.</li><li>5. The browser now sends a request straight to that IP address. The machine living there (Google's servers) responds with all the files and data needed to show the page.</li><li>Every computer on the internet has an IP address. It acts like a postal code: a unique location that lets other computers find it to send or receive files.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In practice your computer and ISP cache DNS answers, so repeat visits often skip the lookup. The request to the server uses HTTP/HTTPS, which the next lecture builds on.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Many sites share a single IP (shared hosting, CDNs). The browser includes the domain name in its request (the Host header) so the server knows which site to return. That is why pasting a bare IP into the address bar does not work for every site.</li></ul>\n<h3>Try it: see a domain's IP address</h3>\n<ul><li>Go to nslookup.io, enter google.com, and it lists the IP address(es) the DNS returns for Google.</li><li>Copy one of those IPs into a new browser tab: the Google home page loads, which repeats by hand what the browser normally does automatically after the DNS lookup.</li></ul>\n<h3>How data crosses oceans</h3>\n<ul><li>Continents are joined by enormous submarine (undersea) cables. submarinecablemap.com shows every one of them and which reach your country.</li><li>Each cable bundles hundreds of optical fibres; lasers send the data as light pulses. The lecture quotes up to 400 gigabytes per second per fibre and shows a cross-section of a cable that lands in New Zealand.</li><li>Because the signals travel at close to light speed, a request can go halfway round the world and back in milliseconds. All the browser needs to start that journey is the server's IP address.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The same DNS lookup from a terminal instead of nslookup.io</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\"># Ask DNS for google.com's IP address(es)\nnslookup google.com\n\n# Shorter output, just the addresses\ndig +short google.com\n\n# See the chain of routers your packets pass through on the way\ntraceroute google.com     # macOS / Linux\ntracert google.com        # Windows</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The request flow as a text diagram</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">You type google.com\n   |\n   v\nBrowser --(&quot;where is google.com?&quot;)--&gt; ISP --&gt; DNS server\n                                            | looks up name\n                                            v\nBrowser &lt;------- IP e.g. 142.250.x.x ---- ISP\n   |\n   +--(request sent directly to that IP)--&gt; Google's server\n   &lt;--(HTML, CSS, JS, images)-------------- Google's server</pre>\n<h2>The task: Try-it: look up an IP address and visit it</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A browser with internet access.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> See for yourself that a domain name is just a label for an IP address.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open nslookup.io.</li><li>Type google.com and run the lookup.</li><li>Copy one of the IPv4 addresses from the results.</li><li>Paste it into a new browser tab's address bar and press Enter.</li><li>Optional: open submarinecablemap.com and find the cables that land in your country.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The Google home page loads from the raw IP address, showing that DNS just converts names into addresses.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking of the internet as wireless or 'in the sky'; it is mostly cables, including under the sea.</li><li>Mixing up the roles: DNS only translates the name into an IP address; the actual files come from the web server at that address.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Expecting every site to load from its bare IP. Sites on shared hosting or behind a CDN usually show an error or a certificate warning that way.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s01-how-the-web-works", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains the three kinds of files a server sends for a web page (HTML for content, CSS for appearance, JavaScript for behaviour) and how the browser combines them to render the page. Then uses Chrome DevTools' Inspect feature to edit text on a live site, and shows why a refresh wipes those edits.</p>\n<h3>Recap from the previous lecture</h3>\n<ul><li>Clients and servers are joined by the internet's wiring. A DNS server acts as a directory that turns a domain name into the server's IP address.</li><li>With the IP address the browser contacts the website's server directly, and the server returns the files the browser needs to draw (render) the page.</li></ul>\n<h3>The three file types and their separate jobs</h3>\n<ul><li>A site is split into several files because each type has a different responsibility.</li><li>HTML = content. It holds what is on the page: text, images, buttons, links. In the course's house comparison, HTML is the bricks, the raw building material.</li><li>CSS = styling. It decides how that content looks: page background colour, rounded button corners, fonts, colours. In the house comparison it is the wall colour and the shape of the door. CSS works by targeting elements created in HTML (a piece of text, a button) and applying styles to them, e.g. make a button red, round its corners and give its label a particular font.</li><li>JavaScript = functionality. It lets the page do things and respond to the user, turning a static page of text and images into something interactive. House comparison: working light switches and a cooker that actually heats, which make the house usable. Real examples: sending an email in Gmail, posting a photo on Instagram.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A useful summary: HTML is nouns (what exists), CSS is adjectives (what it looks like), JavaScript is verbs (what it does).</li></ul>\n<h3>How the browser combines them (Google home page example)</h3>\n<ul><li>A browser is software built specifically to read and render HTML, CSS and JavaScript files.</li><li>Loading the HTML gives the raw components: for Google, one image (the logo), two buttons and a text box for the search term.</li><li>Loading the CSS changes only the appearance of those components (the search box shape, the button colours). CSS adds no new buttons or images; it restyles what HTML already created.</li><li>Loading the JavaScript adds behaviour, so typing a search term and pressing search actually does something.</li><li>Content + styling + functionality together make a modern website.</li><li>Side note from the video: Google used to have an Easter egg where searching 'Google in 1998' restyled the results page to look like the 1998 site. (Extra) It may no longer work.</li></ul>\n<h3>Chrome DevTools: inspecting and editing a live page</h3>\n<ul><li>Right-click any element on a page (e.g. the 'Google Search' button) and choose Inspect. This opens Chrome Developer Tools with the HTML for that element automatically highlighted in the Elements panel.</li><li>Chrome is used because its developer tool suite is one of the best available; this is why the course asked you to install it. Later lessons explore DevTools in more depth.</li><li>For Google's search button the visible label is stored in the value attribute (value=&quot;Google Search&quot;). Double-click that attribute's text, type something new (the video uses 'Angela Search') and press Enter: the button on the page updates straight away.</li><li>The same element also has aria-label=&quot;Google Search&quot;. aria-label is read by screen readers (assistive technology) and is not shown on screen, so editing it changes nothing you can see. Make sure you double-click the value attribute, not aria-label.</li><li>Where the text lives depends on the element. For a news headline (the video uses techcrunch.com) the visible text is the content between the opening and closing tags, displayed in black in the Elements panel. Double-click it to rewrite the headline. This works on any site (BBC News etc.).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The Elements panel shows the live DOM, the browser's in-memory version of the page, which is why edits appear instantly. View Page Source (Cmd+Option+U / Ctrl+U) shows the original HTML file the server sent.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why the edits disappear on refresh</h3>\n<ul><li>DevTools edits only change your local copy of the page inside your own browser. Nothing is sent back to the site's server.</li><li>Refreshing asks the server for the HTML, CSS and JavaScript files again, so the page re-renders from the originals and your changes are gone.</li><li>To make pages that permanently say, look and behave the way you want, you write and host your own HTML, CSS and JavaScript, which is what the rest of the course teaches.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Simplified version of the Google search button seen in DevTools. 'value' is the visible label; 'aria-label' is only for screen readers.</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; name=&quot;btnK&quot; value=&quot;Google Search&quot; aria-label=&quot;Google Search&quot;&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- After double-clicking the value text in DevTools and editing it: --&gt;\n&lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; name=&quot;btnK&quot; value=&quot;Angela Search&quot; aria-label=&quot;Google Search&quot;&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A headline: here the visible text is the element's content, not an attribute</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h2 class=&quot;post-title&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;a href=&quot;/2026/10/some-story/&quot;&gt;Original headline text&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;/h2&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The three layers in one tiny page: HTML content, CSS styling, JavaScript behaviour</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!-- HTML: content --&gt;\n&lt;button id=&quot;btn&quot;&gt;Click me&lt;/button&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- CSS: appearance --&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;\n  #btn { background: red; color: white; border-radius: 8px; font-family: Georgia, serif; }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- JavaScript: behaviour --&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\n  document.getElementById(&quot;btn&quot;).addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n    alert(&quot;The button did something!&quot;);\n  });\n&lt;/script&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Try-it: edit a live website with Chrome DevTools</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Google Chrome open on google.com (and optionally a news site such as techcrunch.com or bbc.co.uk/news).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Change visible text on a real website using Inspect, then see why the change is only local.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Go to google.com in Chrome.</li><li>Right-click the 'Google Search' button and choose Inspect. DevTools opens with that button's HTML highlighted.</li><li>Find the value=&quot;Google Search&quot; attribute and double-click the text inside the quotes.</li><li>Type a new label (e.g. your name + ' Search') and press Enter. Check the button on the page now shows it.</li><li>Note the aria-label attribute: editing that one changes nothing visible because only screen readers use it.</li><li>Open a news site, right-click a headline → Inspect, double-click the headline text between the tags in the Elements panel, and rewrite it.</li><li>Press refresh and watch the original text return.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The edited button label and headline appear on screen immediately, then revert to the originals after a refresh because the browser re-downloads the server's files.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Editing aria-label instead of value on the Google button and concluding that the edit did not work.</li><li>Assuming DevTools changes are saved to the website; they exist only in your browser tab until you refresh.</li><li>Thinking CSS can add new elements. It only changes the look of elements that HTML already created (content comes from HTML).</li><li>Using a browser other than Chrome and finding the Inspect panel looks different from the videos.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l007", "<p class=\"summary\">Study-method advice: watch short chunks for understanding, then rebuild the code from memory; take Cornell-style notes; adjust playback speed but don't skip modules; flag hard topics to revisit; and treat getting stuck as normal, solving it with Google and Stack Overflow.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Lessons are deliberately about 10 minutes each. For longer videos, pause around the 10-minute mark.</li><li>Avoid 'code-along' (typing in sync with the video). Watch the chunk focusing on why each line is written, then go to your editor and reproduce it without looking. Recall forces understanding; copying teaches little.</li><li>The goal is programming skill, not typing speed. If you want faster typing, practise it separately (keybr.com is suggested).</li><li>Cornell notes: one page in four areas: topic at the top, main notes, a side column for keywords/questions/reminders picked out after every few lines, and a summary of the whole lesson in a few bullets at the end. A printable template PDF is in the course resources (also in the reference repo as 01.03 - App Brewery Cornell Notes Template.pdf).</li><li>Use playback speed: 2x for material you already know, 0.5x if the pace is too fast or English is not your first language.</li><li>Don't skip modules; the curriculum ramps up gradually and gaps make later lessons hard. Videos explicitly marked optional/skippable are the exception.</li><li>When a topic is too hard: bookmark it and note the module, retry the watch-then-rebuild method, read around the subject, and come back about a week later. It is usually much easier second time.</li><li>Mastery comes from repeated practice, not from watching once.</li><li>Getting stuck is normal; working developers spend most of their time debugging. Act like a professional: search Google and Stack Overflow, since someone has almost always hit the same problem. Finding the bug is part of how skills grow.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Cornell notes page layout</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">+------------------------------------------+\n| TOPIC                                    |\n+--------------+---------------------------+\n| KEYWORDS /   | NOTES                     |\n| QUESTIONS /  | (most of your writing)    |\n| REMINDERS    |                           |\n| (filled in   |                           |\n|  on review)  |                           |\n+--------------+---------------------------+\n| SUMMARY (a few bullets, after the lesson)|\n+------------------------------------------+</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Typing along with the video at full speed and ending up with working code you can't reproduce.</li><li>Skipping modules that look easy, then missing concepts later lessons build on.</li><li>Giving up when stuck instead of searching for the error or revisiting the topic later.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l008", "<p class=\"summary\">A step-by-step routine for getting unstuck: compare what you expected with what happened, search the error message (Google → Stack Overflow), rewatch the video up to where things broke, diff your code against the downloadable solution, then search or post in the lesson's Q&amp;A using a four-part question format with screenshots.</p>\n<h3>The unsticking routine, in order</h3>\n<ul><li>1. Expectation vs reality: each time you run code, decide beforehand what should happen, then compare it with what did happen and work out what causes the gap.</li><li>2. Read the error message. Errors appear in the browser or the editor (typos, misspellings, failed installs). If the message is cryptic, paste it into Google and open the Stack Overflow results. Professional developers do this every day.</li><li>3. Rewatch the video up to the point where your code broke or started misbehaving, checking for typos and wrong capitalisation along the way.</li><li>4. Compare with the instructor's code: each module ends with a downloadable completed project. Scan it against yours; you can paste in a line or block to test whether it fixes the problem.</li><li>5. Q&amp;A: check the Q&amp;A of the specific lecture where it broke to see if someone had the same issue. If not, post there (not in an unrelated lecture), and include the video timestamp if the question is about a moment in the video.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to write a good Q&amp;A question</h3>\n<ul><li>State what you expected to happen.</li><li>State what actually happened.</li><li>Attach a screenshot of all the relevant code.</li><li>Attach a screenshot of the console/debug output showing any errors or the crash.</li><li>Partial-screen screenshots: Windows: Win+Shift+S, drag the crosshair, and the capture goes to the clipboard. Mac: Cmd+Shift+4, drag the area, and the file is saved to the Desktop.</li></ul>\n<h3>Mindset</h3>\n<ul><li>Getting stuck is a normal part of learning to program; tiny slips such as a missing comma or semicolon can take a long time to find, and solving them builds skill.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Posting a question with no code, no error output and no description of the expected behaviour.</li><li>Asking in the wrong lecture's Q&amp;A, which makes it harder for others to find and answer.</li><li>Ignoring the error text instead of searching it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l009", "<p class=\"summary\">Self-assessment article: pick one of six experience levels and follow a 'Start Here' link to the recommended entry point in the course. The suggestions are optional.</p>\n<h3>Six profiles and where each should start</h3>\n<ul><li>1. Total beginner: start at the very beginning and skip nothing, because each module builds on the previous one. Get comfortable with each module before moving on; about one week per module is the suggested pace. Start link goes to lecture 37348056.</li><li>2. Some HTML/CSS already: begin at the intermediate tutorials and do every challenge. If the challenges feel too hard, drop back to the earlier lessons. Start link goes to lecture 37350296.</li><li>3. Under 6 months of JavaScript/web experience: skip the intro programming modules (dip in only if needed) and complete every lecture with 'challenge' in its title to confirm you can skip the rest.</li><li>4. Experienced programmer, new to web: usually knows some HTML/CSS, so start at the JavaScript modules. Expect JavaScript to have quirks that surprise people coming from stricter languages such as Swift.</li><li>5. Experienced web developer: jump to the back end, starting with Node, then Express, EJS, Postgres and so on.</li><li>6. Experienced web developer who only wants React: go straight to the 8-hour React module aimed at experienced developers (link goes to lecture 17038306).</li><li>These are recommendations only; learners can browse the curriculum and choose their own route.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Beginners jumping ahead to the 'cool' later modules without the foundations, then getting lost.</li></ul>");
